\chapter{Enum Reflection: The Embedded \texttt{magic\_enum} v0.9.8}
\label{ch:enum-reflection}

\section{The problem reflection solves}

A domain-specific language needs to talk about the small closed sets that
dominate its vocabulary: the token kinds of a lexer, the states of a state
machine, the access modes of a file handle, the diagnostic severities of a
compiler front end. In \cpp{} these sets are almost always spelled as
enumerations, because an enumeration gives the compiler a closed set of
values, gives the debugger readable names, and gives the type system a way to
prevent a colour from being passed where a duration is expected.

The trouble is that \cpp{} erases the enumerator names the moment the
translation unit is compiled. A \code{switch} statement can be written by
hand, but nothing in the language lets a program ask, at run time, ``what is
this enumeration value called?'' or, at compile time, ``how many enumerators
does this type have?'' or, at run time, ``given the string \code{"Read"},
produce the enumeration value?'' Language implementations that need this
information \emph{reflect} over the enumeration, recovering the names from
artefacts the compiler leaves behind.

\DSLtk{} does not attempt to solve that problem itself. Instead it embeds a
complete, mature solution: the public headers of \texttt{magic\_enum}
version 0.9.8, byte for byte, and then re-exports that library's API inside
the \code{dsl} namespace. The result is a header-only toolkit that can
reflect over enumerations with no external dependency, no build step, and no
\texttt{magic\_enum::} qualifier anywhere in user code. This chapter
describes what is embedded, how the embedding is exposed, and — most
importantly — the one mechanism that governs everything the library can see:
the bounded search range.

\section{What is embedded, and how it is regenerated}

The embedded region is delimited by two banner comments in
\texttt{DSLtk.hpp}. The first is

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd]
// BEGIN DSLTK EMBEDDED MAGIC_ENUM
\end{lstlisting}

at line 403, and the second is

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd]
// END DSLTK EMBEDDED MAGIC_ENUM
\end{lstlisting}

at line 5816. Between them, in lines 404 to 5815, lie the amalgamated
public headers
of upstream \texttt{magic\_enum} v0.9.8, namely
\code{magic_enum.hpp}, \code{magic_enum_all.hpp},
\code{magic_enum_containers.hpp}, \code{magic_enum_flags.hpp},
\code{magic_enum_format.hpp}, \code{magic_enum_fuse.hpp},
\code{magic_enum_iostream.hpp}, \code{magic_enum_switch.hpp} and
\code{magic_enum_utility.hpp}. The banner also records the provenance
of the block, including the SHA-256 digest of the upstream public headers
and the URL of the tag from which they were taken.

The block is \emph{generated}, not hand-maintained. The script
\texttt{DSLtk/scripts/update-magic-enum.py} performs the amalgamation: it
reads the nine upstream headers, verifies their digest against the recorded
constant, expands the local \texttt{\#include} directives between them into a
single translation unit, and splices the result between the two banner
markers. The script also carries two small local patches, one to keep the
optional string facilities of \texttt{magic\_enum\_containers.hpp}
conditional on the element type being an enumeration, and one to make
\texttt{magic\_enum\_format.hpp} probe for \code{<format>} before the
standard library has been reached, so that \texttt{DSLtk.hpp} can be the
first include in a translation unit. The script's \code{--check} mode
verifies that the embedded block matches the upstream release without
writing anything, which makes the embedding auditable.

Two consequences follow. First, the embedded code is \emph{upstream} code:
it carries its own MIT licence and its own include guards, and it is not
subject to the style of the surrounding \DSLtk{} code. Second, because the
whole block is regenerated from a pinned tag, a user of \DSLtk{} never has to
manage a \texttt{magic\_enum} dependency, never has to reconcile version
conflicts, and never sees a version number of their own.

\section{The \code{dsl} re-export layer}

The \code{dsl} namespace begins at line 5818, immediately after the closing
banner. The first thing it does is create namespace aliases that point at
the embedded library's sub-namespaces (lines 5826--5832):

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
namespace enum_reflection = ::magic_enum;
namespace customize = ::magic_enum::customize;
namespace containers = ::magic_enum::containers;
namespace bitwise_operators = ::magic_enum::bitwise_operators;
namespace iostream_operators = ::magic_enum::iostream_operators;
namespace istream_operators = ::magic_enum::istream_operators;
namespace ostream_operators = ::magic_enum::ostream_operators;
\end{lstlisting}

Because these are aliases and not new namespaces, a user may write
\code{dsl::customize::enum_range<Access>} and have that name refer to
\code{::magic_enum::customize::enum_range<Access>}. Specialising the
alias therefore specialises the upstream template, which is exactly what is
needed.

Next come the \emph{using-declarations} that lift the individual functions
into \code{dsl} proper (lines 5834--5870). They cover the whole public
surface: \code{enum_name}, \code{enum_cast}, \code{enum_value},
\code{enum_values}, \code{enum_entries}, \code{enum_count},
\code{enum_contains}, \code{enum_index}, \code{enum_integer},
\code{enum_underlying}, \code{enum_type_name}, \code{enum_switch},
\code{enum_for_each}, \code{enum_fuse}, \code{enum_next_value},
\code{enum_prev_value} and their circular variants, the flag helpers
\code{enum_flags_cast}, \code{enum_flags_contains},
\code{enum_flags_test}, \code{enum_flags_test_any} and
\code{enum_flags_name}, the case-insensitive tag
\code{case_insensitive}, the subtype selectors \code{as_flags} and
\code{as_common}, the trait helpers \code{is_magic_enum_supported},
\code{is_scoped_enum} and \code{is_unscoped_enum}, and the underlying-type
aliases \code{underlying_type} and \code{underlying_type_t}.

Exactly one thing is \emph{new} in \DSLtk{} and not inherited: the trait
\code{dsl::is_flags} (lines 5872--5887). It answers whether a type is
reflected in flag mode, that is, whether the library will treat its
enumerators as independent bits that may be combined. Its implementation
consults the library's internal subtype computation and compares the result
with \code{as_flags<>}, and it yields \code{false} for any type that is not
an enumeration at all.

Finally, the feature tag \code{dsl::EnumReflection} (lines 5897--5902)
exists so that a DSL class can list enum reflection among its features:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
struct TokenDSL : dsl::DSL<TokenDSL, dsl::EnumReflection> {};
\end{lstlisting}

Its mixin is empty. That is deliberate and it is the same pattern used by
\code{dsl::PatternMatch}: the reflection helpers are free functions in
\code{dsl}, so there is nothing for a mixin to add, and a user who wants
them does not have to inherit from anything.

\section{The search-range mechanism}

\begin{featurebox}[The one thing to understand]
The library can only reflect an enumerator whose underlying value lies in a
finite range. By default that range is $[-128, 127]$. Enumerators outside
it are \emph{invisible} to everything except the unbounded
\code{enum_name<Value>()} form, which recovers a single name directly from
the compiler's own pretty-printed signature.
\end{featurebox}

The range is defined by two macros near the top of the embedded block
(lines 569--580):

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd]
#define MAGIC_ENUM_RANGE_MIN -128
#define MAGIC_ENUM_RANGE_MAX  127
\end{lstlisting}

Both may be redefined on the command line to change the default for every
enumeration in the program. A per-type override is also possible:
specialising \code{dsl::customize::enum_range<E>} replaces the default
bounds for that one type, and adding a static member
\code{is_flags} to the specialisation switches the type into flag mode.
This is precisely what \texttt{DSLtk/examples/25-enum-reflection.cpp} does
for the type \code{Access}:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
template <> struct dsl::customize::enum_range<Access>
{
  static constexpr bool is_flags = true;
};
\end{lstlisting}

Note that the specialisation sets only \code{is_flags}; the bounds remain
the defaults, which is why the enumerators \code{Read = 1},
\code{Write = 2} and \code{Execute = 4} are all within range.

The range matters because the library has no portable way to enumerate the
members of a \cpp{} enumeration at compile time. What it does instead is
\emph{scan} the range: for every integer $i$ in $[\mathit{min},
\mathit{max}]$ it forms the candidate value $\code{static_cast<E>(i)}$ and
asks whether that value names a real enumerator. The test is performed by
the library's \code{is_valid<E, V>} predicate, which asks the compiler for
the pretty-printed signature of the candidate and inspects it. When the
candidate is not a real enumerator, the compiler prints the numeric value
rather than a name, the extractor finds nothing, and the candidate is
rejected. The surviving candidates, in increasing
value order, become the library's canonical \emph{value table} for the type.

The consequences are direct and worth stating plainly.

\begin{itemize}
\item An enumerator whose value is outside the range is not reflected. It
does not appear in \code{enum_values}, it has no name in
\code{enum_names}, it does not contribute to \code{enum_count}, and it
cannot be produced by \code{enum_cast}. No diagnostic is issued: the
enumerator is simply absent. Widening the range for the offending type
restores it.
\item The value table is \emph{ordered by underlying value}, not by
declaration order. This is the ordering that \code{enum_index} uses and
therefore the ordering that \code{dsl::containers::array} uses.
\item For a sparse type, such as \code{enum class Token { Number = -1,
Identifier = 2, End = 7 }}, the library detects the sparsity and switches
to a table-driven representation. For a dense type it uses a compact
representation. The choice is made automatically from the density of the
reflected set.
\end{itemize}

The unbounded form is the exception that proves the rule.
\code{enum_name<Value>()}, where \code{Value} is a template argument rather
than a function argument, does not consult the range at all: it asks the
compiler for the pretty-printed signature of that one value and extracts the
identifier from it. Consequently \code{enum_name<Big::Huge>()} yields
\code{"Huge"} even when \code{Huge} lies far outside the reflected range and
is therefore missing from \code{enum_values}. The upstream comment calls
this version ``much lighter on the compile times'' and the observation is
correct: the unbounded form does not need the range scan, and the range scan
is the expensive part of reflection.

The algorithm is summarised in the
pseudocode block that follows.

\begin{pseudocode}[Range-scanning name lookup]
\textbf{procedure} \code{values<E>()}\\
\quad lo \code{<-} \code{customize::enum_range<E>::min}\\
\quad hi \code{<-} \code{customize::enum_range<E>::max}\\
\quad V \code{<-} empty sequence\\
\quad \textbf{for} i \code{<-} lo \textbf{to} hi \textbf{do}\\
\qquad c \code{<-} \code{static_cast<E>(i)}\\
\qquad \textbf{if} \code{is_valid<E>}(c) \textbf{then}\\
\qquad\qquad append c to V\\
\quad \textbf{return} V\\[1ex]
\textbf{function} \code{enum_name<E, V>()}\\
\quad \textbf{if} \code{customize::enum_name<E>}(V) carries a custom tag \textbf{then}\\
\qquad \textbf{return} that custom string\\
\quad sig \code{<-} the \code{__PRETTY_FUNCTION__} of V\\
\quad \textbf{if} sig contains no enumerator identifier \textbf{then}\\
\qquad \textbf{return} the empty string\\
\quad \textbf{return} the identifier extracted from sig
\end{pseudocode}

Figure~\ref{fig:enum-pipeline} shows the shape of the whole facility: the
source enumeration is scanned over the range, the surviving values and their
names are frozen into a compile-time table, and the call site reads from
that table.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\begin{dslgraph}
    \node[dslbox] (src) {source enum\\\code{enum class Token}};
    \node[dslbox, right=of src] (scan) {range scan\\over \code{[-128, 127]}};
    \node[dslbox, right=of scan] (tab) {name table\\\code{names_v}, \code{values_v}};
    \node[dslbox, right=of tab] (call) {call site\\\code{enum_name<T>()}};
    \draw[dsledge] (src) -- (scan);
    \draw[dsledge] (scan) -- (tab);
    \draw[dsledge] (tab) -- (call);
    \draw[dslfeedback] (src) to[bend right=32]
      node[below, font=\scriptsize] {\code{enum_name<V>()}, unbounded}
      (call);
\end{dslgraph}
  \caption{The reflection pipeline. The solid path is the range scan that
  produces the compile-time value and name tables; the dotted path is the
  unbounded single-value lookup, which bypasses the range entirely.}
  \label{fig:enum-pipeline}
\end{figure}

\section{Compile-time and runtime}

The facility divides cleanly along a line that is easy to miss. The
\emph{shape} of an enumeration --- which enumerators exist, what they are
called, what index each occupies --- is known at compile time, and the
library materialises it as \code{constexpr} data. The \emph{value} of an
enumeration held in a variable is not known at compile time, and the
operations that consume a value are ordinary run-time functions.

That is why \code{enum_name<Value>()} and \code{enum_count<E>()} can be
used inside a \code{static_assert}, while \code{enum_cast<E>(text)} and
\code{enum_name(value)} are ordinary calls:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
static_assert (dsl::enum_count<Token> () == 3);
static_assert (dsl::enum_name<Token::Number> () == "Number");
\end{lstlisting}

The compile-time form is a \emph{template} on the value, so the compiler
evaluates it while instantiating; there is no code generated for it at all.
The run-time form takes the value as an argument, so it must produce the
answer when the program runs, and the lookup is a scan of the frozen table.

The \code{enum_values<E>()} form sits in between. The array it returns is
itself a \code{constexpr} object, so it can be captured in a constant
expression; but the idiomatic use of it is a run-time loop, because the
whole point of materialising the table is to iterate over it:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
for (auto [token, name] : dsl::enum_entries<Token> ())
  std::cout << dsl::enum_integer (token) << ": " << name << '\n';
\end{lstlisting}

\section{A worked example}

Listing~\ref{lst:enum-complete} is a complete, compilable program that
exercises the parts of the facility a user is most likely to reach for: the
compile-time count, the case-insensitive cast from a string, the
compile-time dispatch of \code{enum_switch}, the flag helpers, and the
enum-indexed container.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp, caption={A complete program using the
embedded enum reflection. The static assertions are checked by the
compiler; the remaining output is produced at run time.},
label={lst:enum-complete}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"

#include <iostream>

enum class Token
{
  Number = -1,
  Identifier = 2,
  End = 7
};

enum class Access
{
  Read = 1,
  Write = 2,
  Execute = 4
};

//  Declaring the type as a flag type switches the library into flag mode.
template <> struct dsl::customize::enum_range<Access>
{
  static constexpr bool is_flags = true;
};

struct TokenDSL : dsl::DSL<TokenDSL, dsl::EnumReflection> {};

int
main ()
{
  //  The shape of the enumeration is a compile-time fact.
  static_assert (dsl::enum_count<Token> () == 3);
  static_assert (dsl::enum_name<Token::Number> () == "Number");
  static_assert (dsl::is_flags_v<Access>);
  static_assert (!dsl::is_flags_v<Token>);

  //  The value table, ordered by underlying value.
  for (auto [token, name] : dsl::enum_entries<Token> ())
    std::cout << dsl::enum_integer (token) << ": " << name << '\n';

  //  A name-to-value cast, ignoring case.
  auto token = dsl::enum_cast<Token> ("identifier", dsl::case_insensitive);
  if (token)
    dsl::enum_switch ([] (auto constant) {
      constexpr Token value = decltype (constant)::value;
      std::cout << "Dispatched " << dsl::enum_name<value> () << '\n';
    }, *token);

  //  Flag helpers.
  using namespace dsl::bitwise_operators;
  std::cout << dsl::enum_flags_name (Access::Read | Access::Write) << '\n';

  //  An enum-indexed container.
  dsl::containers::array<Token, int> counts{};
  counts[Token::Identifier] = 42;
  std::cout << "Identifiers: " << counts[Token::Identifier] << '\n';
}
\end{lstlisting}

The program prints

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
-1: Number
2: Identifier
7: End
Dispatched Identifier
Read|Write
Identifiers: 42
\end{lstlisting}

Two details in the listing deserve comment. The first is the
\code{enum_switch} call: the callback is handed an
\code{enum_constant}, that is, a \code{std::integral_constant} whose
\code{value} member is the enumerator itself. Writing
\code{constexpr Token value = decltype (constant)::value;} inside the
lambda recovers a compile-time constant, which is what makes it possible to
use the \code{enum_name<Value>()} form inside the case body. The second is
the \code{using namespace dsl::bitwise_operators;} directive. The
bitwise operators for flag enumerations live in their own namespace so that
they are not found by argument-dependent lookup unless the user asks for
them; the trade-off is that the user must ask.

