Two of these deserve a note.

\code{enum_switch} is not a run-time dispatch table. It expands, at compile
time, into a chain of comparisons against the reflected value table, and it
is usable in a constant expression. Its value is that it is
\emph{exhaustive}: the expansion covers every enumerator the library knows
about, so adding an enumerator to the type and rebuilding produces a
compiler error at the point where the new case is missing, rather than a
silent fall-through. The library can also be configured to emit a real
\code{switch} statement through its hash feature macros.

\code{enum_fuse} deserves more explanation than the table can give. Given
two or more enumerations, it computes a single integer that is a bijective
mix of their values, so that a pair of enumerations can be used as the key
of a two-dimensional dispatch without a nested \code{switch}. The result is
an \code{std::optional} of an opaque enumeration type; the mixed value is
recovered by casting it to \code{std::uintmax_t}.

\section{Enum-indexed containers and bitwise operators}

Two of the embedded library's sub-libraries deserve separate treatment
because they change the way one writes code.

\code{dsl::containers::array<E, T>} is a fixed-size array of \code{T} that
is indexed by an enumeration rather than by a number. Its size is
\code{enum_count<E>()}, and its index mapping is derived from
\code{enum_index}, so it is indexed by the enumeration's \emph{position in
the value table} rather than by its underlying value. For the sparse
\code{Token} type the array has three elements and
\code{counts[Token::Identifier]} addresses the second of them. The
container offers \code{at}, which throws \code{std::out_of_range} for an
unrecognised position, and \code{operator[]}, which asserts. The same
header provides a \code{bitset} over an enumeration, an \code{optional}
over an enumeration, and a \code{set} keyed by enumerations.

\code{dsl::bitwise_operators} provides \code{operator|},
\code{operator&}, \code{operator^}, \code{operator~}
and their compound assignments for flag enumerations. They are deliberately
confined to their own namespace: an unqualified \code{Access::Read |
Access::Write} will not compile until the user writes
\code{using namespace dsl::bitwise_operators;}, which keeps the bitwise
operators from silently hijacking expressions in code that has nothing to do
with flags.

\section{Edge cases and limits}

\begin{warnbox}[Enumerators outside the range are silently dropped]
An enumerator whose underlying value lies outside the interval from
\code{MAGIC_ENUM_RANGE_MIN} to \code{MAGIC_ENUM_RANGE_MAX} is
absent from the reflection. \code{enum_count} does not count it,
\code{enum_values} does not list it, \code{enum_cast} cannot produce it,
and \code{enum_name(value)} returns an empty view for it. The only
diagnostic is the absence itself, which is easy to miss. If an enumeration
uses values such as \code{0x10000}, specialise
\code{dsl::customize::enum_range<E>} for it, or widen the default range
with the two macros.
\end{warnbox}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Duplicate underlying values are not represented twice.} An
enumeration with two enumerators sharing a value has one entry in the value
table. The name that survives is the one the compiler reports for the
value, and the choice is a compiler detail rather than a specified one.

\item \textbf{The value table is ordered by value, not by declaration.} Any
code that assumes declaration order --- a hand-written table of strings
indexed in parallel with \code{enum_values} --- will be wrong whenever the
declaration order and the value order differ. The shipped
\texttt{25-enum-reflection.cpp} illustrates the safe pattern: build the
name and the value from the same iteration.

\item \textbf{\code{enum_value<E>(index)} does not check its argument.} The
upstream comment is explicit: no bounds checking is performed. The
compile-time form \code{enum_value<E, I>()} does check, with a
\code{static_assert}.

\item \textbf{Flag mode is a property of the type, not of the value.} Once
\code{is_flags} is declared for a type, the library treats its enumerators
as bits, and \code{enum_count} counts the bits rather than the
enumerators. Mixing a flag type and a non-flag type in one expression is a
type error, as it should be.

\item \textbf{The flag helpers require the type to be in flag mode.}
\code{enum_flags_name} and friends compute over
\code{detail::enum_subtype::flags} and will not produce a meaningful
result for an ordinary enumeration.

\item \textbf{Name lookups are case sensitive by default.} The
\code{case_insensitive} tag is a comparison predicate passed to
\code{enum_cast} and \code{enum_contains}; it compares ASCII
case-insensitively and is therefore locale-independent, but it does nothing
for non-ASCII characters.

\item \textbf{\code{enum_cast} from text returns an optional.} A failed
cast is not an exception and not a sentinel value; it is an empty
\code{std::optional}. For an enumeration with a zero enumerator, a
successful cast and a failed cast are still distinguishable, because the
optional carries an \code{engaged} flag.

\item \textbf{The reflection is a property of the type, not of the object.}
Nothing here reflects over the members of a class, and there is no facility
for reflecting over the fields of a structure. The only reflection
\DSLtk{} offers is over enumerations.

\item \textbf{Compile-time cost is not negligible.} The range scan is
performed for every reflected type, and it is why the upstream library
recommends the unbounded \code{enum_name<Value>()} form in hot template
code. A translation unit that reflects over many types pays for each of
them.
\end{itemize}

\section{Closing}

The embedded \texttt{magic\_enum} gives \DSLtk{} a complete enum
reflection facility with no dependency, no configuration and no namespace
pollution: the user writes \code{dsl::enum_name} and never learns that the
implementation lives in another namespace. The single concept a user must
carry away is the bounded range, because it is the one thing that can make
reflection silently return less than the user expects.

With reflection in place, the next two chapters turn to the facilities that
manipulate values rather than describe them. \chapref{ch:pipeline} covers
the pipeline feature, in which values flow through a chain of stages;
\chapref{ch:operators} covers the predicate-composition feature, in which
conditions are combined into larger conditions.
